hi this is dr. Gregory Sadler I'm a  professor of philosophy and the  president and founder of an educational  consulting company called reason IO  where we put philosophy into practice  I've studied and taught philosophy for  over 20 years and I find that many  people run into difficulties reading  classic philosophical texts sometimes  it's the way things are said or how the  text is structured but the concepts  themselves are not always that  complicated and that's where I come in  to help students and lifelong learners  I've been producing longer lecture  videos and posting them to youtube many  viewers say they find them useful what  you're currently watching is part of a  new series of shorter videos each of  them focused on one core concept from an  important philosophical text I hope you  find it useful as well chapter three of  ein rands short book the virtue of  selfishness is titled the ethics of  emergencies and in it she's going to set  out what she thinks that that a human  being  ought to be prepared to do for another  human being in emergency situations  so she's considering emergency cases but  she's also providing an argument why  looking at emergency cases is not the  best way to to carry out ethics and to  guide our life so she begins by saying  that many people approach the subject of  ethics by asking such questions as  should one risks one's life to help a  person who is drowning trapped in a fire  stepping in front of a speeding truck or  hanging by his fingernails over an abyss  obviously the last one is quite  hyperbolic but we can think of all sorts  of other situations would you for  example give an organ knowing that  perhaps there's a risk that you'll die  of the surgery to help somebody else or  to help a number of other people  who are about to die otherwise and we  can go on and on and on so you know  these are sometimes quite far-fetched in  hypothetical situations very abstract  and sometimes as in the case of the  philosopher who did in fact jump in and  save a drowning child at the cost of her  own life they present themselves to us  existentially and we have to figure out  what we're going to do so Rand thinks  that the reason why we focus so much on  these he's because of the pervasive  influence of altruism and she says that  it is elevated the issue of helping  others into the central and primary  issue of ethics and this leads in her  view to a sort of false dichotomy which  we're not going to worry so much about  here we're more interested in what she  actually has to say about emergencies  and one of the key points that she is  going to make comes up a little bit  later in the essay after she's talked  about the nature of love and valuing and  stuff like that she says that it's  important to differentiate between rules  of conduct in two different sort of  situations emergency conditions or  situations and normal conditions or  situations and she says this doesn't  mean that we have a double standard of  morality you know that we have a  bifurcated way of dealing with things  there's emergencies over here totally  different set of rules for them and then  normal life over here that would lead to  a kind of compartmentalization that's  that's problematic in ethical life  instead she says no no it's the same  basic principles that are governing our  responses in in both situations but we  need to be able to distinguish between  these kinds of situations so what is an  emergency she says an emergency is an  unsure and unexpected event limited in  time that creates conditions under which  human survival is impossible when she  gives examples  flood and earthquake a fire a shipwreck  we might think about a famine as well we  could come up with all sorts of things  but notice these conditions so it's  unchosen and unexpected limited in time  and it creates conditions under which  human survival is impossible this could  lead to some some issues we might say  well what about situations where  somebody else whether a government or a  super villain or whoever it happens to  be created a situation in which these  conditions were going to result like you  know blowing up a dam and then everybody  gets flooded or you know creating  artificial famine okay that that could  also probably be considered a state of  emergency as well but a state of  emergency is an extraordinary time she  says in an emergency situation the goal  is to combat the disaster escape the  danger and restore normal conditions so  if we're thinking about you know the  drowning child being swept down the  river you don't you know get in the  river and then you know save the child  and then like look around for more  drowning children you know get yourself  a little house in the river and now  you're the the River keeper or something  like that no you save the kid get them  out give them CPR if needed get them dry  and everybody goes on their way and now  the emergency is over so normal  conditions she says are those which are  metaphysically normal normal in the  nature of things appropriate to human  existence so you know what are examples  of this living on land as opposed to  being flooded right and she says by its  nature an emergency situation is  temporary if it were to last human  beings would die and so she says that  it's only in emergency situations that  we should volunteer to help strangers if  it's in our power so she gives example a  person who values human life and is  caught in a shipwreck should help to  save their fellow passengers although  not at the expense of their own life but  this does not mean that after they all  reach shore he should devote his efforts  to saving his fellow passengers from  poverty ignorance  roses or whatever other troubles they  might have nor does it mean he should  spend his life sailing the seven seas in  search of shipwreck effect victims to  save so again strong distinction between  emergency situations which call for  certain kinds of responses and ordinary  situations in which those you know  that's not necessary right those sorts  of responses so she asks who should we  help in emergencies and here is where  you know the the ethics of egoism really  is central here and she says that you  shouldn't be willing to sacrifice  anything whatsoever for anybody she says  let's consider a drowning person is the  person that's drowning a stranger or  somebody that we know if it's somebody  that we're familiar with so it's not  just a blank write a little head bobbing  along arms coming up then we should use  the following way of thinking about  things the risk that we incur should be  proportionate to the value of the person  for ourselves not necessarily the value  of the person for society unless we care  about that so if the president of you  know the United States is again bobbing  down the river I'll throw up I'm  drowning right and you're like well I  don't really care about the political  establishment don't save them according  to ran right unless you think that it  really matters as a matter of fact with  some presidents you might you might be  willing to save up let them drown let's  see who's next in line all right with  other things you know it's going to  depend on what that person's value is to  you and the amount of risk that you take  should be proportionate to that so she  says for example if it's the man or  woman ones one loves then one can be  willing to give one's own life to save  that person why for the selfish reason  that life without the loved person could  be unbearable and so she says the same  principle applies to  relationships among friends you know if  we can do things to help them and we  value them then that's a selfish act on  our part because we like having the  friend in good condition and doesn't  necessarily have to be emergency things  it could be helping them out with money  problems right giving them time giving  them attention all of those sorts of  things what 