with their own he just recognizes the  fact that his own life is the source not  only of all his values but his capacity  to values so we can extend this she says  respect goodwill towards other human  beings and we say other people are a  value because they're the same species  as our self so she says it's on the  ground of that generalized goodwill and  respect for the value of human life that  one helps strangers in an emergency and  only in an emergency and only to the  degree that it's it's still safe for us  to do so all right because that  generalized respect and goodwill does  not compel us to risk our own lives or  even you know take on certainty of  destruction or maiming or something like  that to help others so the general idea  here running throughout this is that  emergencies do furnish us cases where we  should act to help others to some degree  within certain limits depending on their  value to us but we should not be taking  emergencies as the criteria by which we  work out our ethics Rand thinks that  that is a fundamental mistake we need to  see emergency situations as precisely  that emergency situations that we handle  differently but we handle differently  using the same ethical principles as we  would for our ordinary as she calls it  normal conditions of life  you
 

